\section{What the table shows}{

\strong{Coefficient rows.} \code{B} is the posterior median, \code{SE} the posterior
MAD SD, and the interval an equal-tailed credible interval (header
\verb{95\% CrI}). See \emph{Posterior summaries and intervals}.

\strong{Random-effects rows.} Multilevel fits (\code{stan_glmer}, \code{brm} with
grouping terms) report their random effects as a block: the posterior
median SD and credible interval of each component, from the draws, with
no likelihood-ratio line in the note.

\strong{AME.} Draws-native: \code{\link[marginaleffects:avg_slopes]{marginaleffects::avg_slopes()}} computes the
marginal effects per posterior draw, and the table reports their
posterior median, MAD SD and credible interval (equal-tailed or HDI, as
\code{ci_method} says).

\strong{Fit statistics.} By default, \code{n} and the Bayesian R-squared; see
\emph{Fit statistics}.
}

\section{Values of the shared arguments}{

\describe{
\item{\code{ci_method}}{The default gives the equal-tailed credible
interval. \code{"hdi"} (Bayesian fits only) gives the highest-density
interval instead, the shortest interval that holds \code{ci_level} of the
posterior draws (Kruschke 2015), with the header \verb{95\% HDI}. Unlike
the equal-tailed interval the HDI is not transformation-invariant,
so under \code{exponentiate = TRUE} it is recomputed on the exponentiated
draws rather than transformed. \code{"profile"} and \code{"boot_percentile"}
are refused: a posterior has no profile likelihood and no bootstrap
replicates.}
\item{\code{standardized}}{\code{"posthoc"}, \code{"basic"} and \code{"smart"}, for
fixed-effects fits: \code{stan_glm}-style \code{stanreg} models and
standard-formula \code{brm()} models, whose design matrix is recovered
through \pkg{insight}. They are exact affine rescales of the
link-scale summaries: the median, MAD SD and credible bounds all
scale by the same positive SD ratio, and the beta stays on the link
scale under \code{exponentiate = TRUE}. The Gaussian family divides by
SD(y); every other family standardizes the predictors only, the
frequentist \code{glm} convention. The scale factors do not depend on the
engine, so the same model fitted with rstanarm or brms gets the same
betas up to sampling noise. See \emph{What is refused, and why} for the
other cases.}
\item{\code{exponentiate}}{All quantities are computed from the
exponentiated draws directly: the SE is the posterior MAD SD on the
ratio scale, not a delta-method approximation.}
\item{\code{show_columns}}{The group presets (\code{"all_b"}, ...) expand
without the p-value and t columns. \code{"pd"} adds the probability of
direction (Makowski et al. 2019), defined in the note. \code{"rhat"},
\code{"ess_bulk"}, \code{"ess_tail"} and \code{"mcse"} add per-coefficient
diagnostics in an all-Bayesian table; see \emph{Diagnostics}. \code{"p"} and
\code{"ame_p"} have nothing to fill: refused as atomic tokens, dashed in
a table that mixes Bayesian and other fits.}
\item{\code{show_fit_stats}}{\code{"r2_bayes"}, \code{"elpd_loo"}, \code{"looic"} and
\code{"waic"}. See \emph{Fit statistics}.}
\item{\code{vcov}, \code{cluster}}{\code{"classical"} only: the displayed
uncertainty comes from the posterior draws. Model the clustering
with group-level terms instead.}
}
