\section{Posterior summaries and intervals}{

\code{B} is the posterior median and \code{SE} the posterior MAD SD, the scaled
median absolute deviation: the pairing of Gelman, Hill & Vehtari (2020)
and of rstanarm's own print, equal to the posterior SD for a normal
posterior. The interval is an equal-tailed credible interval
(\verb{95\% CrI}), or the highest-density interval under \code{ci_method = "hdi"}
(\verb{95\% HDI}). There is no p-value and no t-statistic, and so no stars
and no \code{p_adjust}. The probability of direction, \code{"pd"}, is the opt-in
posterior summary closest to them.

The AME and the standardized betas follow the same logic: each is
summarized from its draws, by the median, the MAD SD and the credible
interval of \code{ci_method}.
}

\section{Diagnostics}{

Every table is backed by an automatic check of the sampler: R-hat >=
1.01, ESS below 100 per chain, floored at 400 so that fewer chains
never weaken the bar, divergent transitions, E-BFMI < 0.2. The R-hat
and ESS bars of 1.01 and 400 are those of Vehtari et al. (2021,
pp. 671-672). A problem adds a line to the note and raises a warning of
class \code{spicy_bayes_diagnostics}, nested under \code{spicy_caveat}, so
\code{withCallingHandlers(spicy_bayes_diagnostics = ...)} mutes the check
alone. A clean fit prints nothing.

The columns \code{"rhat"}, \code{"ess_bulk"} and \code{"ess_tail"} report the
diagnostics of each coefficient in an all-Bayesian table, as does
\code{"mcse"}: the Monte Carlo standard error of the displayed posterior
median. It tells how many digits a table can show: a displayed digit is
Monte-Carlo stable when twice the MCSE stays below it (Gelman, Vehtari
& McElreath 2026, Section 11.6). Under \code{exponentiate = TRUE} the MCSE
is recomputed on the exponentiated draws.

Only MCMC fits are read: a fit with \code{algorithm = "meanfield"} or
\code{"optimizing"} is refused; refit with \code{algorithm = "sampling"}.
}

\section{Fit statistics}{

\itemize{
\item \code{"r2_bayes"}: the posterior-median Bayesian R-squared (Gelman et
al. 2019), shown by default with \code{n}.
\item \code{"elpd_loo"} and \code{"looic"}: the PSIS-LOO expected log
predictive density and its deviance-scale twin (Vehtari et al.
2017), on request, a few seconds per model. The note gives the
standard error.
\item \code{"waic"}: the Watanabe-Akaike criterion, on request; PSIS-LOO
is generally preferred.
}
An unreliable estimate adds a caveat to the note instead of being
silenced: a PSIS-LOO Pareto k above the sample-size-specific threshold
\eqn{\min(1 - 1/\log_{10} S, 0.7)}{min(1 - 1/log10(S), 0.7)} of Vehtari
et al. 2024, the bound \code{loo::loo()} prints, or a WAIC p_waic above 0.4.
Compare models with \code{loo::loo_compare()} outside the table.
}

\section{What is refused, and why}{

Each refusal is a classed error.
\itemize{
\item Likelihood-based fit statistics (\code{"aic"}, the pseudo-R-squared
rows, ...): a posterior has no likelihood-based information
criterion.
\item \code{p_adjust}, and the \code{"p"} column: there are no p-values.
\item A robust or cluster \code{vcov}: a posterior has no sandwich analog.
Model the clustering or the heteroskedasticity instead
(\code{rstanarm::stan_glmer()}, brms group-level or distributional
terms).
\item \code{ci_method = "profile"} and \code{"boot_percentile"}.
\item \code{nested = TRUE}.
\item \code{standardized = "refit"}, which would re-run the sampler, and
\code{"pseudo"}. Also refused: multilevel fits (\code{stan_glmer}, \code{stan_lmer}
and \code{brm()} with group-level terms), whose beta would need an
explicit decomposition of SD(y); the \code{stanreg} subclasses that are
not GLMs (\code{stan_polr}, \code{stan_betareg}); and brms formulas with
distributional, multivariate or special terms (\code{mo()}, \code{s()}, ...).
In these cases, standardize the predictors before fitting.
\item Non-MCMC fits (see \emph{Diagnostics}).
}
}

\examples{
# rstanarm samples the posterior, which takes a few seconds. The
# example runs in an interactive session only, so that the package
# checks never run a sampler.
if (interactive() && requireNamespace("rstanarm", quietly = TRUE)) {
  fit <- rstanarm::stan_glm(mpg ~ wt + hp, data = mtcars,
                            refresh = 0, seed = 1)
  print(table_regression(fit))

  # Highest-density intervals and the probability of direction.
  print(table_regression(fit, ci_method = "hdi",
                         show_columns = c("b", "se", "ci", "pd")))
}

}
